/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  flash17_two_liquids_reactive -- CHEMISTRY AND TWO LIQUIDS AT ONCE.

  Until this case the engine offered one or the other.  The reactive path
  (speciation, electroneutrality, a solved pH, admitted solids) carried ONE
  liquid; the molecular path carried up to two liquids and no chemistry.  A
  benzene/water feed with a weak acid in it needs both, and this is the case
  that has both: vapour + a SPECIATED aqueous liquid + a benzene-rich organic
  liquid, solved together at 313.15 K and 0.35 atm.

  WHAT THE STUDENT SEES

      V/F = 0.324                       -- a third of the feed leaves as vapour
      organic = 1.68 % of the backbone liquid moles, 96 % benzene
      ln(a_org / a_aq) = 2e-15          -- the equality that TIES the two
                                           liquids, printed, not assumed
      pH = 2.89                         -- the acetic acid speciating in the
                                           aqueous phase, unaffected by the
                                           organic one holding no water

  The organic phase is small and it decides everything.  Without it the same
  20 kmol/h of benzene sits in the aqueous backbone at a mole fraction near
  1e-4 with an activity coefficient near 1e3, and the equilibrium pressure of
  that liquid comes out near 16 atm instead of 0.36 -- so the saturation
  pre-check waves the case into a Newton looking for a vapour that is not
  there.  Two liquids are not a refinement here; they are the difference
  between a right answer and a wrong one.

  WHERE IT SITS.  The feed's three-phase bubble pressure is 0.357 atm, so
  0.36 atm gives a SUBSATURATED two-liquid state with no vapour at all, and
  below about 0.347 atm the vapour has stripped enough benzene that the
  organic liquid disappears and the case becomes an ordinary reactive flash.
  This case sits between those, in the window where all three phases exist.
  Move P and the phase set follows -- announced, both ways.

  THE 0.0845 kW, AND WHY IT IS NOT ZERO.  This flash is ISOTHERMAL, so the
  duty that holds 313.15 K is a real, computed quantity: Q = 0.0845 kW.  It
  is small because the feed enters at the flash temperature and the vapour it
  makes is mostly benzene, whose latent load is nearly paid for by the acetic
  dimer association (-0.0024 kW, priced exactly) and by the mixing terms --
  not because the case is thermally neutral.  The by-unit energy ledger then
  reports dH = 0.0869 kW against declared items of 0.0845 kW, a 0.0024 kW
  gap, and it is REPORTED rather than rounded away.

  What that gap is NOT: it is not the convergence residual of the flash.  The
  two are different quantities and the case prints both, which is the point.
  The equilibrium solve is judged on a NORMALIZED residual (system/solverDict
  declares the contract; `src/solver/Convergence.H` defines it), and the run
  prints five numbers for it -- the raw residual at the seed and at the
  answer, the normalized residual at both, and the achieved reduction --
  followed by the criterion that decided.  For this case:

      raw   |r|1   6.79e-02  ->  1.28e-09     (log-form, dimensionless)
      normalized   9.18e-03  ->  1.66e-10
      reduction    1.81e-08
      criterion    absolute tolerance (normalized residual <= tolerance)

  So the flash is converged on a stated criterion, and the 0.0845 kW duty
  beside it is physics, not slack.  A dimensional number is never a verdict
  on its own -- it is a magnitude, and a magnitude means nothing until it is
  compared with the magnitude of what is being balanced.  Ruling:
  `docs/design/normalized-residual-convergence.md`.

  THE APPROXIMATION, declared and announced every run: the organic phase's
  membership is DECLARED, not computed.  What is excluded is not the ~0.2
  mol% of water benzene really holds; it is that the water-benzene
  immiscibility CAUSING the split is asserted rather than predicted, so
  ethanol's partition between the liquids rests on two binaries with no
  ternary term.  UNIFAC prices both liquids -- the same model on both sides,
  because the coupling is an equality of ACTIVITY and two models would make
  the residual measure their disagreement instead of the distance from
  equilibrium.  UNIFAC is PREDICTIVE, an estimate: no pair was regressed for
  this system, and the run says so.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoSolve;
description  "Reactive flash with TWO liquids: vapour + speciated aqueous + benzene-rich organic at 313.15 K / 0.35 atm -- V/F = 0.324, organic 1.7 % of the liquid, pH 2.89";
verbosity     3;

reports
{
    streamTable    { }
    massBalance    { }
}
